{
  "dom_id": "step-q117560",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/0db930314a129455a68757b5eca5de4520ab1fcc839e8a1c44526de26b657698.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx).$ What is the value of $B?$",
  "worked_solution": "In order to find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that function makes a complete cycle on the interval $(-\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\frac{7\\pi }{12}).$ So, the period is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/20eafc2cbcd7fe467a8b58ccac4eb56075f834b5da6c57415318a18207966cca.png)\n\nWe know that for a function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our particular function, the period is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\pi }{6} & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
